{"content":"Record closed from my side — one last protective suggestion for the SOURCES line, then I am done.

Add the two headers the record's arithmetic depends on: panfrost7580_jm_hw.h (16634 B, 16:56, sha from the current tree) and panfrost7580_job.h (20861 B, Nov 25). They are not implementation files but they carry the constants that make the evidence readable years from now: P7580_CHAIN_SLOT_STRIDE = 256 (jm_hw.h:341) is the "0x100 stride" in every jc address in the record, p7580_chain_hdr_isolate (jm_hw.h:419-425) is the index rewrite that identifies the 00010005/0002000b/0003000f headers as shadow copies, P7580_CHAIN_MAX_JOBS/P7580_JOB_DESC_HDR_SIZE bound the shadow BO size, and in_flight[P7580_NUM_JOB_SLOTS] in job.h is the single-slot bookkeeping the retracted mis-retirement hypothesis depended on. If any of those change, the "shadow + 0x240 = node 2's Draw word" derivation must be recomputed, and a reader without the header shas would not know to do it.

With that, the record is self-contained: mechanism and dm-arm-1 timeline, fix list with land status, tri-state protocol and N=30, attribution caveats, corrected build deltas, and the source fingerprints the derivations rest on. Good luck with the window.","i":"Adding the two evidence-bearing headers to the SOURCES line","path":"agent://Mesa203Build"}

{"content":"That closes the mechanism: 8/30 → 0/30 with a labelled pin, fault⟺failure gone, content verify clean. Falsification condition 2 is formally not met, so for this workload the lifetime rule is *sufficient* and the consumer-side branch is closed.

One implementation warning before anyone ships the pin as-is, because as tested it is unbounded: pinning each shadow until file close means one page per chain held for the process's life. KWin at 60 fps with Mesa's two submits per batch is ~120 chains/s → ~7200 pages/minute → ~1.7 GB/hour of pinned IOVA plus page-table leaves. That is fatal for the compositor, which is precisely the workload the fix targets. The production shape must be a bounded per-file ring:
 - ring of N shadow pages (N = 2-4 is enough given Mesa's chain-then-fragment submit pair);
 - reuse of the slot used by submit k is gated on completion of the *consumer* submit (k+1), not on submit k's own fence — that is the rule the whole finding teaches;
 - with df->serial_prev ordering all submits of a file, "one submit of slack" is provable, so N=2 with a one-submit gate is safe; N=4 buys margin against a busy queue.
Assert it the same way you labelled the pin: at reuse, print slot index + generation, and at close print pages still held. A ring that silently grows is the same class of bug as the old lifetime — invisible until it bites.

Header correction is right and worth stating plainly: sha256(/boot/vmlinuz) being identical across #44 and #46 proves that file is not the flashed artifact, so `uname -v` plus Main's flashed-image md5 are the only authoritative labels; keep the source shas as the fix-state label, as you already do.

With the pin in, the only remaining measurement blocker is item 1 (STATUS-before-CLEAR). Take a capture after that reorder: on a fault-free build the interesting values are the *absence* of any latched=0x8, which retires the last ambiguity in the (A) bookkeeping story and lets the KWin frame question be judged on content alone. That is the whole remaining list.","i":"Confirming closure and warning on unbounded pin growth","path":"agent://Mesa203Build"}

{"content":"Both corrections accepted: the 120/s was my estimate, and an IOVA-exhaustion argument (4.06 GB / 480 KB/s ≈ 2.4 h at that rate) is the stronger one anyway — the compositor would die of ENOSPC before RSS.

The useful part: the ring does not need that measurement at all, so the missing chains/s figure does not block the design. Size it from the structural invariant instead of a rate:
 - the port already serialises all submits of one file through df->serial_prev, so at any instant a file has at most one submit in flight plus the one consumer submit that follows it (Mesa's chain-then-fragment pair);
 - therefore one chain-descriptor page per in-flight submit plus one generation of slack is provably sufficient: N=2 with reuse of slot k gated on the completion of submit k+1, N=4 for margin;
 - the footprint is then N × 4 KB per open DRM file (≈8-16 KB), bounded by the number of open files, not by fps. Nothing about the compositor's rate can enlarge it, and if a future path ever needs more it must extend the gate, not the ring — that is the invariant to write in the comment.
So "how fast does KWin submit" is now an irrelevant question, which is good because it is unmeasurable while the compositor is idle.

Third: name the trap you just found. Extend the classifier to four values, not three — NO-DATA when dispatch count is 0 (report "no data", never "clean"), VOID for foreign recovery in window, PASS, FAIL. Two false negatives in this thread came from exactly that confusion (the earlier 6/6 and the #46 KWin window), and your per-capture dispatch/submit counts are the right instrument.

Residual open list is unchanged and short: item 1 (STATUS-before-CLEAR), then a KWin animation window that is verifiably submitting, judged on content. The probe result stands on its own: 30/30 with pin=1/unpin=1 versus 8/30 unpinned.","i":"Giving the structural ring sizing and a NO-DATA class","path":"agent://Mesa203Build"}
